"anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Edmonds–Karp, rather than Dinic, because each of its steps is one picture: a shortest path in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the residual network and the flow sent along it. Dinic’s blocking flows interleave advancing,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"retreating and pruning within a level graph, which do not read as separate steps."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Making the sequence numbers the vertices and reads the capacities as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"edmondsKarpMaximumFlow(from:to:capacity:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" does; each iterator builds its own residual"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"network and runs that call’s searches, in the same order, so the flows it sends are that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"call’s: the pushes less the cancellations on each edge are its "},{"code":"flow(ofEdgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"bottlenecks add up to its "},{"code":"value","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". The sequence holds no copy of the graph; its positions stay"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"valid until the graph is mutated. Stopping the loop stops the algorithm."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The Edmonds–Karp algorithm as a sequence of ","type":"text"},{"code":"EdmondsKarpEvent","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s, pulled one at a time.","type":"text"}],"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Structures","title":"Structures","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Flows\/documentation\/Flows\/EdmondsKarpEvents\/Iterator"]},{"anchor":"Instance-Methods","title":"Instance Methods","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Flows\/documentation\/Flows\/EdmondsKarpEvents\/makeIterator()"]},{"anchor":"Type